rom the rules you approve.\n\n## 6. Data protection (UK GDPR)\n\n6.1 For your customers' and staff's personal data, **you are the controller and Keystone is your processor**. We process it only to provide the services and only on your documented instructions (this agreement and Schedule 2 are those instructions).\n\n6.2 We will:\n- keep personal data confidential and make sure anyone who handles it is bound to confidentiality;\n- use appropriate security (access controls, encrypted connections, least-access accounts, logs);\n- use only the sub-processors listed in **Schedule 2**, give you 30 days' notice of any new one, and let you object;\n- where data leaves the UK (for example some US-hosted tools), rely on the UK's approved safeguards (an adequacy decision, the UK International Data Transfer Addendum or the UK Extension to the EU-US Data Privacy Framework);\n- tell you **without undue delay, and within 48 hours**, after we become aware of a personal data breach, and help you meet your own reporting duties;\n- help you answer requests from people exercising their data rights, and with impact assessments where reasonably needed;\n- at the end of the agreement, return or delete your personal data within 30 days (unless the law requires us to keep it), and confirm this in writing;\n- give you the information you reasonably need to show we meet these terms, and allow a reasonable audit once a year on 30 days' notice.\n\n6.3 You confirm you have a lawful basis to give us the data and that your privacy notice tells your customers about the tools you use (including AI phone answering and call transcription).\n\n6.4 We keep call logs, quotes and job records no longer than needed: by default **12 months for call logs and 24 months for quotes**, unless you ask for a different period or the law requires longer (for example accounting records you keep in Xero).\n\n## 7. Halal-only client policy\n\n7.1 Keystone only works with businesses whose main activities are halal. We do